AB  - Out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization is a critical challenge in machine learning, especially for tabular data where real-world distributions often differ from training data. TabPFN is a recent transformer-based model that achieves strong performance on small tabular classification tasks by training only on synthetic data. However, its ability to generalize to OOD scenarios in real-world datasets has not been fully explored. In this work, the evaluation of the pre-trained TabPFN model is extended to the UCI Adult dataset, focusing on OOD splits based on education, age, and gender. TabPFN’s performance is compared with traditional models such as Random Forest, XGBoost, and LinearSVC. Experiments show that TabPFN consistently achieves higher accuracy and F1 scores on OOD samples across different demographic splits. Visualizations and statistical analysis are provided to support these findings. This study demonstrates the practical value of TabPFN for robust tabular classification in the presence of distribution shifts, and highlights its potential for real-world applications where fairness and reliability are important.
