AB  - In recent years, numerous convolutional neural network (CNN) models have been proposed to achieve a high performance. Among them, YOLOv8 is a suitable model for edge devices with limited hardware resources because YOLOv8 is designed with consideration for deployment on edge devices such as consumer electronics. However, CNNs in edge devices must be robust against out-of-distribution data, and their robustness against out-of-distribution data has not been sufficiently verified. This study verifies the robustness of YOLOv8 for out-of-distribution data. We measured the out-of-distribution accuracy of lightweight models trained on ImageNet running in real time on the YOLOv8 backbone and edge devices. We also measured shape bias, the tendency to emphasize shape when categorizing, because it is known to be related to the robustness of out-of-distribution data. We trained YOLOv8 with ImageNet and evaluated it based on the out-of-distribution accuracy and shape bias. The experimental results show that the YOLOv8 backbone has a lower out-of-distribution accuracy than the other lightweight models. Moreover, the shape bias may facilitate the learning of feature representations that are useful for downstream tasks.
ER  - 

