AB  - The performance of graph neural networks often deteriorates significantly when faced with the problem of out-of-distribution generalization, where the test data exhibits distribution shifts from the training data. Recently, several efforts have been made to improve the graph out-of-distribution generalization on homophilic graphs. However, it is still questionable whether existing methods can be effective on heterophilic graphs. To address these challenges, we propose a multi-path gated graph neural network for node-level out-of-distribution classification tasks in heterophilic graphs. Specifically, to effectively suppress irrelevant neighborhood information and reduce spurious correlations, we present a multi-path dynamic gating module composed of k parallel aggregators, in which each path adaptively regulates the feature propagation process through learnable gating functions. Additionally, a graph-level path contrastive loss function is proposed to learn distinct node environment distributions for each path by minimizing inter-path feature distribution similarity and maximizing intra-path feature consistency. Furthermore, we propose an environment probability module-based dynamic fusion mechanism to capture causal ego-subgraph features in heterophilic graphs by adaptively fusing the node features from k paths. Extensive experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed method.
