AB  - Graph neural networks (GNNs) have achieved remarkable success in node classification tasks, yet their performance significantly degrades when encountering out-of-distribution (OOD) data due to spurious correlations learned from biased training environments. This fundamental limitation mirrors a broader challenge in artificial intelligence: while humans demonstrate exceptional adaptability to novel situations through evolutionary mechanisms, current models remain trapped within in-distribution (ID) learning paradigms, mechanically fitting statistical patterns rather than understanding underlying causal mechanisms. Existing graph-invariant learning methods attempt to address this through hierarchical environment inference, but suffer from critical limitations including error accumulation across network layers and computationally intensive deployment requirements. In this article, we propose node-level invariant learning on graphs with mixup (NodeIGM), a novel framework that introduces direct causal subgraph inference to eliminate error accumulation while achieving superior deployment efficiency. Our key innovation lies in a learnable causal edge discriminator that directly separates causal and environmental subgraphs without layerwise dependencies, combined with strategic environment mixup for robust invariant learning. During training, our framework jointly optimizes the GNN encoder, causal edge discriminator, and classifier through unified loss computation and backpropagation. During inference, only the GNN encoder and classifier are required, reducing model parameters by approximately 50% and accelerating inference speed by  $2\times $ – $3\times $ . Extensive experiments on multiple benchmark datasets demonstrate that NodeIGM achieves state-of-the-art performance while significantly enhancing deployment feasibility for real-world applications. Our code is available at https://github.com/Brucesustech/NodeIGM.git
ER  - 

