AB  - The unprecedented advancement of generative AI has led to Deepfake images that are increasingly realistic and perilous. Modern GANs, autoencoders, and diffusion models can make human faces, change identities, and change expressions with a level of accuracy that has never been seen before. Their arrival has permanently damaged trust in digital images and put biometric authentication systems to the test. This study thoroughly examines image-based Deepfakes, emphasising their four principal manipulation axes and the ramifications they present for forensic analysis. It suggests a single taxonomy of detection methods that includes classical machine learning, deep neural networks, GAN-inspired methods, Vision Transformers (ViT), and strong hybrid architectures. To add to this, the main datasets and metrics that make up current benchmarking are put together. A critical review of recent advances reveals a raft of open issues: dataset bias, vulnerability to adversarial attacks, lack of cross-domain generalization, and the rapidly evolving nature of generative models. Finally, the paper points out some promising ways that Deepfake detectors could be made stronger, easier to understand, and more useful in the future. In this way, it gives a timely reason to move forward with the science of digital image forensics.
ER  - 

