AB  - Reliable perception is critical for downstream motion planning, yet most drivable-area segmentation systems output deterministic masks that may become overconfident and unsafe under adverse conditions (e.g., fog, blur, glare, and sensor noise). This paper presents a lightweight, safety-aware perception framework that augments segmentation with uncertainty maps and a risk-aware boundary output. Built upon an efficient encoder-decoder backbone, we instantiate epistemic uncertainty via Monte Carlo dropout and deep ensembles, and further improve probabilistic reliability through post-hoc temperature scaling. We derive pixel-wise predictive entropy and mutual information to localize uncertain regions, then construct conservative risk boundaries by uncertainty thresholding followed by morphological erosion, producing safety-oriented drivable masks suitable for constraint-aware planning. Experiments on a controlled synthetic benchmark with multiple corruption types and multiple random seeds show that uncertainty-aware inference substantially reduces OOD performance degradation compared with deterministic baselines, while calibration consistently improves expected calibration error without sacrificing segmentation accuracy. Qualitative results confirm that uncertainty concentrates around ambiguous boundaries and occlusions, and the proposed risk-boundary mechanism effectively suppresses high-risk areas to yield more conservative and interpretable outputs.
ER  - 

