AB  - Vision Language models (VLMs) such as LLaVA have shown impressive capabilities in visual reasoning but remain vulnerable to spurious correlations and biased learning patterns inherited from pre-training datasets. Existing debiasing approaches either rely on costly human annotations or are tightly coupled to specific architecture, limiting their scalability to modern VLMs. This paper presents a bias-reshaping strategy that improves robustness and generalization in multimodal reasoning without introducing additional supervision or model parameters. The proposed approach integrates token-conditioned logit-guided bias estimation, a pseudo-label debiasing function, and adaptive focal-style reweighting with light adversarial textual perturbation. Experiments on LLaVA and LXMERT demonstrate consistent gains on out-of-distribution (OOD) evaluation while maintaining in-distribution (ID) accuracy. Our results suggest that efficient bias reshaping is a practical path toward trustworthy multimodal intelligence at LMM scale.
ER  - 

