AB  - Conventional multimodal learning typically relies on paired and synchronized data to enforce alignment across sensory domains, an assumption that can limit scalability and data flexibility in practice. This work examines whether independently trained unimodal decoders, optimized under shared symbolic objectives and alphabets but without paired supervision, can nonetheless converge to compatible outputs. Two Convolutional Recurrent Neural Network models with Connectionist Temporal Classification loss were trained separately on the Text-and-Audio CAPTCHA dataset, which encodes identical alphanumeric strings through distorted images and synthetic speech, using disjoint training splits for each modality. Both models exhibited stable convergence without overfitting, achieving an accuracy of 0.985 with a character error rate (CER) of 0.0027 on images, and 0.800 accuracy with a CER of 0.062 on audio. A pooled performance summary across 10,000 test instances reports an average accuracy of 0.893 and CER of 0.032, reflecting aggregate task performance rather than explicit cross-modal alignment. While no direct alignment metrics are evaluated, the results indicate that independently optimized decoders can exhibit compatible symbolic mappings under shared decoding constraints. The study provides a reproducible baseline for analyzing empirical consistency in unpaired multimodal settings and serves as a controlled testbed for future investigations of alignment, complementarity, and privacy-compatible learning strategies.
ER  - 

