AB  - Generalizing robotic manipulation skills to unseen objects and heterogeneous platforms remains a bottleneck in embodied intelligence due to the shortcut learning inherent in 2D visual policies. We propose Spatial-DiT, a spatial multimodal diffusion policy framework designed for robust zero-shot generalization. Our core contributions include: 1) a Multi-scale Pyramid Encoder (MPE) coupled with a Multi-scale Fusion (MSF) module that extracts texture-invariant geometric features from point clouds; 2) a Delta 6D Pose action representation that ensures action continuity and enables platform-agnostic skill transfer; and 3) a Diffusion Transformer (DiT) architecture for high-fidelity multimodal action generation. Extensive real-world experiments on xArm6 and Kenbei platforms demonstrate that Spatial-DiT achieves an 88.3% average success rate across novel instances and categories. Notably, the policy exhibits remarkable robustness to transparent and reflective objects without fine-tuning, showcasing its potential for scalable, cross-embodiment deployment in unstructured environments.
