AB  - Deepfake generation technology evolves continuously, rendering static detectors obsolete whenever a new generative model is released. This brittleness stems from a structural limitation: closed-set classifiers learn decision boundaries only for generators seen during training and possess no mechanism to recognize or recover from distributional shift at deployment time. We formalize this failure mode as open-set continual detection and address it with AFSA (Autonomous Few-Shot Prototype Adaptation), a framework that decouples shift detection from adaptation without retraining the encoder. The central contribution is a margin-based drift detector: the decision margin m(z) = dreal − dfake collapses toward zero when an embedding enters a prototype-uncovered region, providing a geometry-driven, label-free signal that triggers diversity-aware selection of K support samples and a closed-form prototype bank update in 12 ms with no gradient computation. Across two open-set scenarios cross-paradigm (GAN→Diffusion) and within-family (StyleGAN2→StyleGAN3) AFSA recovers from 47.2% zero-shot accuracy to 96.5%–99.9% with as few as K = 5 labeled samples, and 96.8%–99.9% at K = 20 (AUROC 0.987–0.998, FPR/FNR < 5.6%). Encoder fine-tuning at 32× greater computational cost yields strictly lower accuracy (94.4%), confirming that the frozen-encoder design is preferable in the low-data regime. Ablation studies confirm that each of the four framework modules generator-specific prototypes, drift detection, diversity-aware selection, and EVT open-set rejection contributes independently and is non-redundant.
