AB  - Glaucoma is a leading cause of irreversible blindness affecting over 70 million people worldwide. Multitask learning integrating segmentation information has been widely adopted in automated glaucoma diagnosis based on the assumption that explicit anatomical knowledge enhances classification performance. However, systematic validation across diverse clinical datasets remains limited. We systematically evaluated segmentation integration effects by comparing five learning strategies across 19 datasets containing over 12,000 fundus images through rigorous cross-validation and cross-domain analysis. Despite achieving high segmentation accuracy, integration provided minimal classification benefits. Only a single dataset showed statistically significant improvement after correction for multiple comparisons, with negligible AUC gain. Cross-domain validation revealed systematic generalization failure: models trained on 18 datasets and tested on held-out datasets demonstrated no significant improvements across all conditions, with universally poor segmentation transferability. Notably, the dataset showing substantial within-dataset improvement exhibited complete cross-domain failure with near-random discrimination, demonstrating that observed benefits represented dataset-specific artifacts rather than genuine algorithmic advantages. These findings challenge widespread assumptions about anatomical knowledge integration in medical AI, emphasizing the necessity of empirical validation over assumption-based design.
ER  - 

