AB  - Low-resolution image classification (e.g., CIFAR and Tiny-ImageNet at $32 \times 32$) faces persistent challenges due to limited contextual diversity, overfitting, and vulnerability to adversarial perturbations. Conventional augmentations provide only superficial variability, while recent LLM-guided diffusion approaches, though semantically rich, suffer from computational inefficiency and resolution mismatch. To address these challenges, we present SEGA-Net, a novel semantic-enhanced augmentation framework that bridges this gap through two key innovations. First, SEGA-NET introduces a lightweight text-tolatent projector that maps LLM-derived prompts to the latent space of a frozen class-conditional GAN, enabling efficient and semantically controlled $32 \times 32$ image synthesis. Second, it implements a security-aware filtering pipeline combining CLIP-based semantic alignment, SSIM de-duplication, and blur detection to ensure that only high-fidelity, label-consistent samples are used for training. Operating in three stages, (i) LLM-guided prompt generation, (ii) projector-steered GAN synthesis, and (iii) quality-filtered augmentation, SEGA-NET achieves substantial improvements across five benchmarks (CIFAR-10/100, SVHN, Tiny-ImageNet, and Fashion-MNIST) and four architectures (ResNet, MobileNetV2, VGG-16, and ViT). Compared to conventional augmentations, SEGA-Net delivers $\boldsymbol{+} \mathbf{8} \boldsymbol{-} \mathbf{1 0 \%}$ gains in clean accuracy and adversarial robustness (FGSM, PGD, BIM, AutoAttack), while reducing generation cost by $6.7 \times$ relative to diffusion-based methods. These results demonstrate that semantic-guided synthesis with explicit quality control significantly enhances robustness and efficiency for resourceconstrained vision tasks.
ER  - 

