AB  - This paper introduces a CV-first pipeline that delegates image parsing to specialized modules and reserves reasoning for a frozen language model, enabling efficient zero-/few-shot recognition and visual QA without multimodal pretraining. The system composes object tags, attribute phrases, and diverse captions into structured textual scene descriptors, which a general-purpose LM consumes to perform classification and grounded question answering across standard CV benchmarks. Extensive experiments show competitive or superior performance to larger end-to-end multimodal baselines on object recognition suites and vision–language tasks, supported by ablations isolating the contribution of tag/attribute signals and caption diversity. Implementation details cover encoder choices, decoding strategies, and hardware settings, with limitations analyzing dependency on upstream detectors and context-window constraints, positioning the approach as a scalable alternative for CV systems seeking multimodal capability without joint alignment training.
